&nbsp;

{# lede #}<span class="text-white">Enterprise Jamstack Wiki Platform</span>&nbsp; for <b>creating, managing, and delivering</b> static webpages using Jamstack Technology for dynamic features therein{# lede #}. For pricing information, see our [Orion Plans](plans).

&nbsp;

### Here's the ROI, Executive Decision Maker :fa-chart-line:...

&nbsp;

<span class="text-white">Why Choose Orion? The Jamstack Wiki Platform that Outshines &mdash; and Costs Far Less</span>

&nbsp;

What truly sets Orion apart isn't just another wiki platform &mdash; it's our proprietary Orion Technology, a battle-tested, patent-pending approach that delivers an elegant user experience with unmatched security, reliability and efficiency.

&nbsp;

<span class="text-white">Nothing will yield better ROI than Orion in the Knowledge Management System market.</span>

&nbsp;

Unlike the products your competitors are stuck using, Orion is a framework designed to yield superior, maintainable, confidential yet easily locatable corporate documentation with the richest, yet most comprehensible, managed link/dependency network on the market. It turns your organization’s collective experience from a liability that leaks away into your most durable competitive advantage.

&nbsp;

<div class="accordion" id="how-so-container" style="background-color: transparent !important; color: black">
<div class="accordion-item" style="background-color: transparent !important; padding-bottom: 0px; border: none">
<div class="accordion-header" id="how-so-heading">
<button class="accordion-button collapsed" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#how-so-target" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="how-so-target">

#### How so?

</button>
</div>

<div id="how-so-target" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="how-so-heading" data-bs-parent="#how-so-container" style="background-color: transparent !important; border: none; color: black">

&nbsp;

Adopting Orion immediately reduces your risk of a major security incident; and the peace of mind delivered matters more to your board than an [expensive ransomware insurance policy](https://thedfirreport.com/2025/02/24/confluence-exploit-leads-to-lockbit-ransomware/) you never need to file a claim against.

Setting aside the security advantages, a fundamental flaw of wiki-like products other than Orion is that they utterly fail to provide **coherent integrations/interfaces for incorporation and periodic maintenance of externally-generated data file formats**. However, by focusing on a handful of industry standard text/source document/data formats, even your most intricate data-generated tables, graphs, charts, maps, and architectural diagrams are **fully searchable and AI translatable** in any of Orion's supported natural languages.

&nbsp;

For example, you will see **no more lost productivity** for staff trying to acquire periodically updated, critically confidential infosec cloud asset configuration details :fa-network-wired: locked in an obscurely tagged, externally generated data table somewhere in your present wiki, and in frustration reaching out to teammates who also don't recall where it is. Which naturally escalates the evolving time sink to managers; and so it goes. Until someone remembers, and because the table is huge and you just want the contents of one element in a single row, you have to fetch and eyeball scan it manually. Then repeat the process whenever you discover that the infosec data is next updated[^HTML5].

&nbsp;

Since Orion searches version-controlled sources instead of ephemerally built pages, your staff will efficiently locate the true permalink originals of the materials they seek- not confusingly amass artificially elevated hits buried in a dozen different peripheral copies of the sought-after information.

&nbsp;

That externally generated infosec table :fa-network-wired: gets incorporated into your Orion wiki as a **searchable `CSV` file** attached to an underlying markdown document, which otherwise renders it at build time into an embedded `HTML5` table.

&nbsp;

Want to be notified when that attached `CSV` is altered? Simply subscribe to the `CSV` file! Upon notification during your next visit to the wiki (and not via email spam), you can skip the embedded `HTML5` rendered markdown table and instead directly fetch exactly what you need from Orion's `JSON` build of the `CSV` attachment using standard `CLI` tools like `curl` and `jq`.

&nbsp;

**Timeliness, convenience, accuracy, and precision, in the endless persuit of the current single source of truth, function as major productivity enablers for your most time-constrained employees**. The likely consequences of not having these Orion-only features is that your Platform Engineers will be **forced to waste $10-100K annually on makework** by creating, building, and supporting miniature, internal websites with `REST`-based interfaces to these shared datasets, as augmentation workarounds for your wiki vendor's limited searchable and retrievable data integration feature set.

&nbsp;

Contrarily, Orion will surface your once-lost, externally generated infosec details :fa-network-wired: on demand, and **render them in a manner amenable to the automatable web-enabled/AI agents that your talent prefers**. With unparalleled alacrity and reliability. Want to perfect your search by hand tuning to taste, until you ultimately prefer `JSON` to `HTML5` in your results? Again, Orion delivers (same `URL`, different `Accept` header.)[^refinements]

&nbsp;

In other words, **information retrieval** is a fundamentally different problem domain than content creation/modification, but the CRUD-based wiki apps from our competitors do not make this distinction in a meaningful way. Additionally, with Orion's Jamstack design, taxonomies aren't tacked on as an unmanageable, generic/emergent dynamically generated afterthought culled from overtaxed `SQL` tables. Instead, they are informed with careful structural planning and consistently enforced, heirarchical organization by experienced information architects across your team and ours[^sprawl].

&nbsp;

Next, **content curation tooling** is an essential feature of Orion, not an opportunity to upsell. Our per-wiki facts database is an industry first &mdash; handy for things like managing consistent name branding or related software artifact metadata across the entire website with a 1-line edit to a [YAML file](https://github.com/SunStarSys/www.iconoclasts.blog/blob/trunk/lib/facts.yml).

&nbsp;

So instead of the inevitable, entropic link rot innate to other wiki products, you get **internal/external link validation** at page modification time during a comprehensive, `MIME`-type based, pre-commit linting pass by Orion's `CMS` editor. We also provide every customer with a reporting infrastructure to **catch stale internal/external links on demand**, even when the pages involved haven't been altered in months. Diagrams are just as easy to keep in sync with evolving alterations to the markdown prose, because with Orion's mermaid integration, **the diagram sources are embedded in the page's prose** itself. Painless recursive search / replace to repair the accumulated cruft is built into the `PCRE` search engine in the `CMS`.

&nbsp;

Your wiki is no longer of deteriorating utility for your teams; but becomes **a rewarding resource for them to use and enhance.**  Also, you don't need to be a wiki administrator to use these tools &mdash; almost anyone can voluntarily participate in the grooming process, or perhaps be organizationally delegated to perform this duty as team leads. Measurable ROI growth across your organization is a follow-on, continuous blooming effect of the aggregate gardening and caretaking abilities unique to our wiki platform.

&nbsp;

Moreover, as your site evolves to the point where files or folders need to be relocated or deleted, Orion not only tracks location moves by deploying automated `HTTP` redirects from the original locations to the new targets, but also continues to enshrine into rule the existing `ACL`s on the original locations in perpetuity. This ensures your confidential content remains protected against historical searches from unauthorized users in the system who might otherwise have direct access to Orion's version control interfaces to your wiki's sources.

&nbsp;

Ever seen a system that can reliably integrate filtered content/data across independent source files?  [**Orion can**](https://www.iconoclasts.blog/joe/), with full suppport for the [**cyclic cross-dependencies**](https://www.iconoclasts.blog/joe/dependencies) and authorization controls on each source file.  There's simply no way to accidentally (or maliciously) launder access-controlled content through its content integration functionality, and there's no way for content/data in such files to be modified without those modifications percolating down to induce concurrent builds of their dependent files[^application].

&nbsp;

Of course none of this is possible with products like Notion because it is impossible to reseed an existing generated database with an updated external file import; you have to delete and recreate the entire database (or possibly use third party tooling). By deleting the original database, you also **destroy all the wiki links and associated user watch notification lists for it**. "Unpleasant" doesn't begin to cover it.

&nbsp;

Besides, `YAML/JSON` aren't even supported data ingestion formats in Notion!  If you can't shoehorn externally generated tree-like data into a `CSV` table, all you have left are bare page blocks with which you can waste more time better spent mastering our Orion platform.

&nbsp;

There's nobody else in the industry who has put this much refinement into every aspect of their wiki product as a total package.

&nbsp;

Futhermore, Orion scales with you as you grow, especially at the enterprise level. The $0 per seat licensing and unlimited number of websites opens up new opportunities for controlled knowledge sharing and collaboration across teams, partners, websites, and even the entire planet with our federated platform! We know that for most clients, just a tiny portion of those seat licenses would be used to actually edit pages, but they can use an unlimited number of "associated" read-only accounts in their wikis to share protected information with over the internet. Accounts that need not have any access to the Orion `CMS` proper- a cost-saving feature unique to Orion as the only corporation-backed **Enterprise Jamstack Wiki**.

&nbsp;

As your personnel's corporate status evolves over time, Orion makes it easy for wiki administrators to deal with the wiki reauthorizations involved. Doing a mass search and replace on every explicit `ACL` setting on the site that's tied to an ex-employee's account is as trivially automatable as onboarding a new staffer or upgrading an existing one by dropping them into the appropriate version-control authorization groups that your team designed[^ownership].

&nbsp;

With other market solutions, [without a premium plan you are on your own](https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence/resources/guides/premium-admin/manage-content-at-scale#what-is-content-manager).

&nbsp;

Finally, we tailor every installation to your specific requirements, and are **always available for consultation and professional coaching** to ensure you maximize returns on your investment in our products and services. **Your team has a partner in us** in terms of ensuring you see the anticipated productivity gains and customer satisfaction associated with Orion &mdash; designed for robust, verified, collaborative knowledge creation/curation; and easy, comprehensive, precision information retrieval thereof.

&nbsp;

##### Footnotes

&nbsp;

[^HTML5]. To add insult to injury, it turns out your existing wiki only renders page contents as an implicit javascript-based web browser application, instead of Orion's explicit standards-compliant `HTML5` document. This implies, among other things, that even if your wiki's search engine surfaces the tables you need, you can't directly access those tables as agentic downloadable wiki content unless the infosec team goes through the trouble of consistently deleting and re-exporting the table (as a `CSV` attachment) whenever the data is updated.

&nbsp;

[^refinements]. Didn't immediately find what you were looking for in Orion's search hits? Refine your current search with a search filter on the material you did locate; repeat if necessary. Or click on a suggested associated keyword generated from the located material to shift your seach in an adjacent direction. Or expand the scope of your search to a higher level in the list of directory breadcrumbs at the upper end of the page. Or switch to a `PCRE` regex search instead of the Lucene natural-language-stemmed bareword match popularized by Google in its formative years.

Does your current solution offer your staff this level of exploratory search capability? Definitely not if it's Confluence or Notion.

&nbsp;

[^application]. Applying this technology to the infosec table :fa-network-wired: situation: your team could use it to integrate and filter the Orion-pemalinked `CSV` attachment into its own separate wiki page, and Orion will ensure the content on that separate page is updated whenever the permalinked infosec table :fa-network-wired: is, not just when the wiki page is edited by hand. As is the case with other wiki solutions.

&nbsp;

[^sprawl]. Orion has many tools for controlling wiki sprawl; from building dynamic topological constraints into the site-wide `ACL`s, to simply supporting **document archival** &mdash; which is a document [`Status`](api/doc#-code-status-code-5) setting that signals to Orion that the page should be elided from its dependency management graph; effectively unlinking it from all document indexes and cross-references, and instructing Orion to ignore it in `CMS` directory listings, while preserving the file as a searchable permalink and archiving a copy of it to a corresponding current year/month timestamped location elsewhere on the wiki.

&nbsp;

[^ownership]. In fact, there is **no formal concept of page ownership** in the system. Orion's version control simply tracks the per-user-authorized changesets of staff at the time of the user-committed changes, lending to a more natural, collegial corporate acculturation towards the combined work product of a wiki than in any other similar system. Locking down pages to individual user accounts/"authors" is anathema to the collaborative ideals of wikis, but it is certainly possible moving forward with explicit `ACL` header assignments on the pages involved.

&nbsp;

